From: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: PPPoE upload encap behind a 
 bench-only ingress alias

Uploads come from the switch, whose head tag the parser cannot walk past
with the reset TPIDs, so they are not offloaded in service. Kamil
Bienkiewicz's ingress alias for rtl8_4 (his 0445) makes them parse, at
the cost of the tagged WAN's downloads (measured by him: they never hit).

Add it as bench profile A, on top of profile E: the ingress and tunnel
parser TPIDs set to S 0x8899 / C 0x0000, the CPU port made an S-VLAN
egress port re-emitting both tags unmodified (without it the flood path
to the CPU rebuilds a lone 0x8100 tag and the LAN dies, his
measurement), and the conduit's parser role core, last; saved, read back
and restored in reverse. It is applied only by writing "A <seconds>" (at
most 600) to /sys/kernel/debug/ppe/offload_profile with offload on, and
reverted by "E", the timer, disable and unload; every flow is deleted
when it comes and goes, and it comes or goes only when all of them were
(an entry stuck in hardware keeps the profile it was programmed under).
Tagged ingress is declined while it holds, and an offer checks under the
lock that the profile did not change since its parsing was judged.

With it, accept the upload of a PPPoE flow, from the conduit under A
only, whatever the board's ports (its encapsulation is a bench
hypothesis): FLOW_ACTION_PPPOE_PUSH plus the VLAN push towards the WAN
port, translated to the PPP interface's address, addressed to the
session's concentrator; the nexthop points at the session's egress L3
interface, which carries the session ID and PPPoE encapsulation, with
the PMTU at most the PPP MTU. As uploads keep hitting on the conduit's
L3 interface, a session that goes out of hardware has its L3 interface
cleared, and read back, only after its flows are deleted; a stuck upload
then meets an MTU of 0 and is redirected to the CPU, and its slot is
retired. Under A an upload is accepted only when its client addresses
the conduit's MAC (its bridge's or its port's): the conduit's L3
interface routes only frames to its own MY_MAC entry.

Profile A exists to check on this gateway what the tagged WAN does under
the alias and to exercise the upload encapsulation. While A has the
parser's TPIDs (and if it is kept after a failed revert) no 802.1Q tag
is followed either, so edma_port_feature_check() turns checksum and
segmentation offload off for tagged frames until they are back;
synchronize_net() lets transmits already past the check finish queueing
before the TPIDs change; frames already in a Tx ring, or requeued after
a full ring, may still leave with a bad checksum in that switch-over. A
is refused while degraded, as a failed restore may have left its values
in place. The pppoe_offload description and the comments that called
uploads impossible say so.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
